      "text": "First, we identify the arithmetic sequence given in the problem. The first term $a = 1$, and the common difference $d = 6$. Using the general formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have:\n\n\\[ a_n = a + (n-1)d \\]\n\\[ a_n = 1 + (n-1)6 \\]\n\nWe need to determine the term that yields a 259. Setting $a_n = 259$, we have:\n\n\\[ 259 = 1 + (n-1)6 \\]\n\\[ 259 = 1 + 6n - 6 \\]\n\\[ 260 = 6n \\]\n\\[ n = 43 \\]\n\nNow, we find the sum of this arithmetic series. \n\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a + a_n) \\]\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (1 + 259) \\]\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{43}{2} (1 + 259) \\]\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{43}{2} \\times 260 \\]\n\\[ S_n = 43 \\times 130 \\]\n\nNext, we compute the remainder when dividing $S_n$ by $6$:\n\n\\[ 43 \\implies \\mod 6 \\]\n\\[ 130 \\implies \\mod 6 \\]\n\\[ 43 \\times 130 \\implies \\mod 6 \\]\n\nWe know that $43 \\equiv 1 \\mod 6$ and $130 \\equiv 4 \\mod 6$. Therefore,\n\n\\[ 43 \\times 130 \\equiv 1 \\times 4 \\equiv 4 \\mod 6 \\]\n\nHence, the remainder when the sum $1 + 7 + 13 + 19 + \\cdots + 253 + 259$ is divided by $6$ is $\\boxed{4}$.",
